      "writtenOn": "2026-09-02T20:27:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Just to confirm this is a way to avoid doing all variations of the check like are_radius_check_predicates_valid does with the three `(rad \u003c\u003d max - min) \u0026\u0026 (min + rad \u003c\u003d max) \u0026\u0026 (max - rad \u003e\u003d min)`.\n\nI haven\u0027t read exactly what AlmostEqual does but I assume it adds some tolerance to the comparison. This is probably more then enough tolerance for our uses cases to be safe, but less generally robust then just doing the three variations of the math/comparison as was done before.\n\nAnyways I\u0027m fine with going with this new patter, I just want to confirm my understanding",
      "parentUuid": "a1496020_75c1c5c6",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 777,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 779,
        "endChar": 74
      },
      "revId": "b22cfafb4fed6f19addc3f5fe3f3a8432814f7fa",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "dc23284f_c53e9e8c",
        "filename": "src/core/SkRRect.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 779,
      "author": {
        "id": 4623515
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-09-09T19:52:56Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "AlmostEqual does add some tolerance to the comparison using ULP (units of least precision). They are essentially the smallest \"difference\" between two representable floating point values. Kaylee explains it in this CL (https://skia-review.git.corp.google.com/c/skia/+/1279236), but you can think of it as a dynamic way of measuring tolerance depending on the magnitude of the two numbers being compared.\n\nThe AlmostEqual check is different from the below checks in that the below is a fast path for verifying \n* sum \u003c\u003d limit (thus checking the relative distances)\n* minCoord + radiusFromMin \u003c\u003d maxCoord - radiusFromMax (thus checking the absolute distances)\n\n\nAnd when floating point imprecision leads to the above to evaluate to \u003e rather than \u003c\u003d, then we fallback to using AlmostEqual and SkScalarNearlyEqual",
      "parentUuid": "5fd0f0da_2998819b",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 777,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 779,
        "endChar": 74
      },
      "revId": "b22cfafb4fed6f19addc3f5fe3f3a8432814f7fa",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
